  "narrative_body": "Russia jammed the GPS on the RAF aircraft carrying John Healey, the defence secretary, for the entire three-hour flight back from Estonia — the Ministry of Defence called it the most dangerous Russian action against the UK since 2022.\n\nThe attack came the day after it emerged that two Russian warplanes had repeatedly and dangerously intercepted an RAF spy plane over the Black Sea the previous month. Whether Mr Healey was personally targeted is unknown; the flight path was visible on public aircraft tracking websites. A similar incident targeted his predecessor, Grant Shapps, in 2024, suggesting a pattern of electronic harassment directed at UK defence ministers travelling near the front. Mr Healey condemned it as \"unacceptable.\"\n\nHe was in the region for the NATO foreign ministers meeting in Helsingborg, Sweden, where the American administration delivered a different kind of threat to the alliance. The Pentagon formally told allies it planned to shrink the pool of military capabilities available under the NATO Force Model during a crisis or conflict. The announcement coincided with the chaotic cancellation and then reinstatement of US troop deployments to Poland and Germany. Marco Rubio, the American secretary of state, attempted to reassure allies; Mark Rutte, the NATO secretary general, said the drift toward less American reliance was \"to be expected.\" European allies began quietly planning alternative structures. For the UK, which holds the GIUK Gap and spends more on defence than any European ally except France, the message from Helsingborg was blunt: fill the gap.\n\nNot all the week's security signals pointed in the same direction. UK, American and Australian defence ministers were preparing to announce a collaboration on uncrewed underwater vehicles at the Shangri-La Dialogue in Singapore — the first concrete deliverable under AUKUS Pillar Two, which has until now lacked focus projects. Australia separately committed A$11 billion to extend its Collins-class submarines. Pillar Two is now operational in at least one domain; the broader submarine readiness picture, with most of the Royal Navy's Astute-class fleet in low readiness, remains unaddressed.\n\nThe week's most consequential development may yet prove to be diplomatic rather than military. The American president described a US-Iran deal as \"largely negotiated,\" with a 60-day ceasefire reportedly in its final stages. Keir Starmer welcomed the progress publicly, calling for \"unconditional and unrestricted\" freedom of navigation through the Strait of Hormuz and stating Iran must never develop a nuclear weapon. A ceasefire and Hormuz reopening would cut oil prices by roughly half — stripping out the primary driver of the inflation and borrowing pressures currently consuming the government's political capital. Darren Jones, the chief secretary to the Treasury, was careful to say a peacekeeping mission was \"not on the table right now,\" while noting that RFA Lyme Bay with minehunting drones is already positioned at Gibraltar. The coalition posture has been built for exactly this transition, but no deal is yet done.\n\nIn the meantime, the economic vice is tightening. Rachel Reeves announced what she called a \"Great British Summer Savings\" package: a temporary VAT cut from 20% to 5% on tickets to family attractions and children's meals, free bus rides for under-16s in August, a fuel duty freeze cancelling planned September and December rises, import tariff cuts on over 100 food items, and a new tax on oil companies routing profits through foreign branches to reduce their UK corporation tax bill. The Treasury expects \"hundreds of millions\" annually from the loophole closure. An earlier plan to cap supermarket prices was quietly dropped after retailers and banks objected. Ms Reeves opened her Commons statement by citing Q1 GDP growth of 0.6%, the fastest in the G7 — but Bloomberg's verdict was blunter: the economy had \"turned sour\" for businesses even as the headline number flattered.\n\nThe underlying picture explained why. Andrew Bailey, the Bank of England governor, told the Treasury Committee that rate cuts had been \"effectively taken off the table,\" the Iran war \"the dominating change in the landscape.\" CPI fell to 2.8% in April from 3.3% in March, partly because of the Ofgem cap reduction, but Mr Bailey warned the July cap rise would push it back up. April public borrowing came in above expectations — the highest since COVID — narrowing the chancellor's already thin fiscal headroom. The Reeves package was carefully constructed to avoid testing the gilt market, funded by targeted tax measures rather than new borrowing: a political intervention disguised as fiscal prudence.\n\nWhether it shifts the political weather is another question. Labour is polling at 17%. The Makerfield by-election on 18 June — the structural clock governing the leadership contest — shows Andy Burnham leading Reform UK's candidate by just 3 points, 43% to 40%, with Restore Britain's candidate at 7%. Elon Musk endorsed Restore Britain on X, prompting Nigel Farage to warn he was \"trying to split the right\" and that Mr Burnham would be \"delighted.\" Mr Farage has his own problems: the Parliamentary Standards Commissioner is investigating a £5 million gift from Christopher Harborne, a crypto billionaire, which Mr Farage claims predated his return to Parliament and needed no declaration. He also claims his phone was hacked by Russian state actors who then fed the story to the media — a claim his colleague Robert Jenrick defended publicly. A separate County Court judgment from 2024 for an unpaid debt of £9,400 remains unsatisfied on the public record. Mr Farage says it was sent to the wrong address and he is appealing.\n\nEven as the succession machinery grinds toward its 18 June test, a new faultline has opened inside the Labour Party. David Lammy, the foreign secretary, refused five times on live television to say whether the UK should rejoin the EU. Analysis in Politico found Brexit already dominating how Labour's potential successors are positioning themselves: Wes Streeting has called it \"a catastrophic mistake,\" while Mr Burnham has kept his position deliberately vague. Mr Starmer, for now, is not going anywhere — his spokesman and Mr Lammy both confirmed no departure timetable would be set. But the Washington Post called the UK \"seemingly ungovernable,\" Bloomberg assessed that \"Starmer's Lame Duck Status Risks Policymaking Paralysis,\" and the gap between the government's stated intent and its capacity to act has rarely been wider. The Makerfield result, three weeks away, will determine whether it widens further.",
